<%# One remembered change, as the author stated it. The row says which action it
    remembers, which record it is about, and the values it carries. Beside those it
    says what became of the change, but only once the apply that decided it has
    stored an answer: on an open draft there is no outcome, and a badge guessing at
    one from a version stamp would be a second answer about the same record
    (ADR 0024). %>
<%# `changes` is the collection this row was rendered from, so every row on the page
    shares one `DraftChanges::Reader` and the record and the tag names behind each change
    are resolved once per class instead of once per row — the cost recorded as finding 66.
    It is nil when a caller renders a single change, and the per-row read is back for that
    row alone, so a new caller has to pass it. %>
<%# `draft` is passed only by the draft's own page, and only while it can still be acted
    on; the same row on the change history or a reviewer's page passes none, so those
    surfaces cannot inherit a drop control the controller would refuse. %>
<% changes = draft_changes(local_assigns) %>
<% draft = local_assigns[:draft] %>
<% record = draft_change_record(change, changes) %>
<% attributes = draft_change_attributes(change, changes) %>
<% outcome = change.outcome %>
<div class="list-group-item entity-row draft-change">
  <div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
    <span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-light border"><%= t("drafts.actions.#{change.action}") %></span>
    <% if outcome.present? %>
      <span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-light border draft-change-outcome">
        <%= draft_change_outcome_badge(outcome) %>
      </span>
    <% end %>
    <span class="entity-title">
      <%# A change about a record that has since been deleted is plain text: a
          link to a page that answers 404 would be a dead affordance, and the kind
          beside it still says what the change was about. The record is resolved
          once here, through the reader this page shares, because
          `DraftChange#resolved_record` deliberately does not remember its answer. %>
      <% if record.present? %>
        <%= link_to draft_change_subject(change, record), record.search_url %>
      <% else %>
        <%= draft_change_subject(change, record) %>
      <% end %>
    </span>
    <span class="small text-body-secondary"><%= draft_change_kind(change) %></span>
    <time class="small text-body-secondary" datetime="<%= change.created_at.iso8601 %>">
      <%= l change.created_at, format: :short %>
    </time>
    <%# **Dropping a change is the author's own control on a working draft.** The
        local is passed only by the draft's own page, so the same row partial on the
        change history, a reviewer's page, and the collaborators pages renders no
        control at all — and `draft?` keeps a submitted or closed draft from offering
        one the controller would refuse. The confirm matters because a drop is not
        undoable: it removes the row rather than marking it, so there is nothing left
        of it to re-apply. %>
    <% if draft&.draft? %>
      <%= button_to t("drafts.changes.drop"),
        universe_draft_change_path(draft_id: draft, id: change),
        method: :delete,
        class: "btn btn-sm btn-outline-danger ms-auto",
        data: { turbo_confirm: t("drafts.changes.drop_confirm") } %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <% if attributes.any? %>
    <div class="entity-description">
      <% attributes.each do |label, value| %>
        <div><span class="fw-semibold"><%= label %>:</span> <%= value %></div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <%# A delete carries no payload at all, and a create whose only remembered
        values were the scope column has nothing a reader could use. Both are
        stated rather than left as a row with an empty second line. %>
    <div class="entity-description">
      <%= change.deleting? ? t("drafts.show.removes_record") : t("drafts.show.no_values") %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# Why this change is not live, where it is not. The remembered values above are
      still what the author asked for whatever became of them, so the reason sits
      under them rather than replacing them: a skipped change is still readable, and
      still redoable from the record's own page (ADR 0021). %>
  <% if outcome.present? && draft_change_outcome_reason(outcome).present? %>
    <div class="entity-description draft-change-outcome-reason">
      <%= draft_change_outcome_reason(outcome) %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
